 ## Background
-The Newell, Newell, and Sancha (NNS) algorithm was originally developed for sorting polygons [S5, unofficial]. It was extended to 3-D polyhedra for volume rendering of unstructured grids [S2, S3, S6]. The method is based on the work of Newell, Newell, and Sancha (1972) and Newell (1974) [S1][S5, unofficial]. It serves as a basis for more recent techniques in volume rendering of unstructured data [S5, unofficial]. The algorithm is particularly relevant for visualizing simulation data from curvilinear grids and finite element analysis where cells may be deformed and non-planar [S1, S6].
+The Newell, Newell, and Sancha (NNS) algorithm was originally developed for sorting polygons [S5, unofficial]. It was extended to 3-D polyhedra for volume rendering of unstructured grids [S2, S3, S6]. The method is based on the work of Newell, Newell, and Sancha (1972) and Newell (1974) [S1][S5, unofficial]. An early solution to computing a visibility order given by Newell, Newell, and Sancha (NNS) continues to be the basis for more recent techniques [S5, unofficial]. The algorithm is particularly relevant for visualizing simulation data from curvilinear grids and finite element analysis where cells may be deformed and non-planar [S1, S6].
 
